Parkinson’s Disease Endotype and Therapeutic Discovery Through a Contextualized, Causal, Multi‑Omics Knowledge Graph with Hallucination‑Audited AI Agents

ouadi Maakoul 

Date: 26 May 2026

---

Abstract

Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a syndrome, not a single disease. The recent failure of the Phase 2b LUMA trial — where an LRRK2 inhibitor achieved >90% target engagement yet did not slow progression in unselected patients — starkly demonstrates that therapeutic success demands endotype‑based patient stratification. Current biomedical knowledge graphs (KGs) cannot meet this demand: they store interactions as flat triples, losing the cellular, genetic, and experimental context that determines whether a relationship holds true; they lack rigorous hallucination control, causal filtering, single‑cell resolution, and principled handling of contradictory evidence. This thesis proposes to construct the first context‑aware, causally‑pruned, hallucination‑audited multi‑omics knowledge graph for Parkinson’s disease, specifically designed to support endotype discovery and therapeutic hypothesis generation. No part of this system has yet been implemented; this document is a theoretical blueprint for the entire PhD research programme.

The proposed framework rests on six interconnected conceptual innovations, each filling a documented gap:

1. Context‑quadruple knowledge representation — biological facts are stored as (Entity, Relation, Entity, Context, Source), where Context captures cell type, genetic background, spatial location, assay conditions, and other metadata, enabling condition‑specific reasoning and contradiction resolution.
2. Adversarial hallucination auditing — every extracted quadruple is verified by two independently prompted large language models (LLMs) and must be grounded to its primary source, with the auditor itself benchmarked against existing hallucination‑control methods.
3. Triangulated causal inference — Mendelian randomisation, colocalization, and Steiger directionality tests using the GP2 Mega‑GWAS (1.8 million samples, 134 risk loci) will prune non‑causal edges and assign causal confidence scores.
4. Single‑cell‑ and spatially‑resolved endotypes — vulnerable neuron subtypes (SOX6⁺ AGTR1⁺, SOX6⁺ ANXA1⁺, RIT2‑enriched, Exc5 cortical, and MIRO1‑R272Q‑disrupted cells) and glial states are encoded as distinct node types; spatial transcriptomics data adds anatomical coordinates; graph embeddings will cluster patients into clinically meaningful endotypes, including a prodromal risk index.
5. Calibrated Bayesian hypothesis scoring — every generated hypothesis will carry an explicit posterior probability, evaluated via Expected Calibration Error (ECE) and compared against state‑of‑the‑art uncertainty quantification frameworks (BKG2U, MedBayes‑Lite, Ca2KG, and UAG).
6. Continual learning under coherent task distributions — Prompt‑Aware Elastic Weight Consolidation (PA‑EWC) will enable the knowledge graph to incorporate new clinical trial and literature data without catastrophic forgetting, benchmarked against vanilla EWC and LLM‑based graph continual learning methods.

Validation will be entirely retrospective and in silico: the system must rediscover known PD targets (e.g., LRRK2) from pre‑2010 knowledge snapshots, independently recover the recently described α‑synuclein–ClpP protein interaction, and surface GPNMB as a causal risk mediator with antibody‑repurposing potential. A drug repurposing module and an organoid‑based experimental validation protocol are included as extended, post‑thesis deliverables.

The core thesis is designed to be defensible within 30 months. All planned code, graph snapshots, extracted quadruples, and evaluation benchmarks will be released under open‑source licenses. The proposal is explicitly a theoretical plan; no experimental or computational work has yet been performed, and all timelines, metrics, and architectures are projected.

