1. Background and Problem Statement

1.1 Parkinson’s Disease Heterogeneity and the LUMA Imperative

Parkinson’s disease is clinically defined by motor symptoms, but its underlying biology is vastly heterogeneous. Over 130 independent genetic risk loci have been identified (GP2 Mega‑GWAS, 2025), and monogenic forms are caused by mutations in LRRK2, GBA1, SNCA, PRKN, and PINK1. Pathological mechanisms range from mitochondrial dysfunction and lysosomal impairment to neuroinflammation and protein aggregation.

This heterogeneity has direct clinical consequences. The Phase 2b LUMA trial of BIIB122 (Biogen/Denali), a highly selective LRRK2 kinase inhibitor, tested in 648 individuals with early‑stage PD, failed to meet its primary or secondary endpoints despite >90% peripheral LRRK2 inhibition and ~30% reduction in CSF phospho‑Rab10 (Biogen press release, 21 May 2026). Development in unselected PD has been halted, while a separate trial continues exclusively in LRRK2 mutation carriers. The LUMA outcome is the most compelling contemporary demonstration that target engagement alone is insufficient in a biologically mixed population. A discovery system must therefore partition patients into endotypes defined by the molecular context in which a target is causally active and generate therapeutic hypotheses conditioned on those contexts.

1.2 Single‑Cell Definition of Vulnerable Neuronal Populations

Advances in single‑nucleus transcriptomics and epigenomics have resolved the cellular complexity of the PD midbrain and cortex. The following populations are particularly relevant:

· SOX6⁺ AGTR1⁺ dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) show altered chromatin accessibility at SNCA, LRRK2, and PRKN loci and are the earliest degenerating population (Kamath et al., 2026).
· SOX6⁺ ANXA1⁺ Aldh1a1⁺ neurons, a subpopulation of the ventral tier, are selectively lost before motor symptom onset in MitoPark mice; inhibiting transmitter release from these neurons is sufficient to induce bradykinesia and tremor, defining them as the cellular substrate of prodromal motor dysfunction (PMC11702755).
· RIT2‑enriched SNc neurons express the PD risk gene RIT2 and are selectively vulnerable (Science Advances, 2024).
· Exc5 cortical glutamatergic neurons show cell‑type‑specific transcriptomic changes (Wang et al., 2024).
· MIRO1‑R272Q‑disrupted neurons: The MIRO1‑R272Q mutation impairs PINK1‑mediated mitochondrial arrest, defining a fifth genetically‑encoded endotype already represented in organoid data (Zagare et al., 2025). Recent work (Thayer et al., EMBO J. 2025) demonstrated that diverse mitochondrial damage converges on loss of mitochondrial membrane potential, which blocks PINK1 progression through the TIM23 translocase, causing it to remain bound to TOM on the outer mitochondrial membrane — a unified damage‑sensing mechanism. This establishes a clear molecular chain: TIM23 → PINK1 stabilization → Parkin activation → MIRO1 ubiquitination, conditioned on [loss of MMP]. Such a context‑dependent cascade cannot be represented as a flat triple, directly motivating the context‑quadruple schema.
· Microglia and oligodendrocytes actively contribute to pathology; PD risk genes such as LRRK2 and GBA1 are highly expressed in microglia, and glial‑neuronal interactions mediate α‑synuclein spread.

Any modern knowledge graph must be able to represent interactions at the level of these specific cell types and genetic backgrounds.

1.3 The Necessity of Spatial Context

Single‑cell dissociations lose the anatomical coordinates of where a molecular event occurs. Recent spatial transcriptomics studies in PD substantia nigra provide this missing dimension:

· Ma et al. (Nature Communications, 2025, DOI: 10.1038/s41467‑025‑62478‑3) employed snRNA‑seq, Visium spatial transcriptomics, and T cell receptor sequencing to map CD8⁺ T cell infiltration and astrocytic responses to specific nigral subregions, finding clonally expanded, tissue‑resident CD8⁺ T cells reactive to α‑synuclein and spatially correlated with CD44⁺ astrocytes.
· GeoMx digital spatial profiling has distinguished PD from multiple system atrophy transcriptomic patterns (Shin et al., Acta Neuropathologica Communications, 2025).
· MERFISH has resolved dopaminergic neuron subtype distribution in LRRK2‑G2019S mice with subcellular precision (PMC12068872).

Incorporating spatial coordinates into the knowledge graph’s context will allow the system to answer questions such as “Is this interaction occurring in the ventral tier where ANXA1⁺ neurons reside?” — directly relevant to the prodromal endotype.

1.4 Knowledge Graphs and Their Current Limitations

Biomedical knowledge graphs store biological entities as nodes and relationships as edges. Recent PD‑focused systems include:

· So et al. (2026): explainable KG identifying TPP1 as a target; static, flat triples.
· Akgüller et al. (2025): multi‑modal GNN for drug repurposing; no causal filtering.
· Zagare et al. (2025): PD organoid‑derived KG; flat triples, no hallucination control.
· BioMGNN v2 (2026): heterogeneous GNN with patient‑patient graph; no quadruples, no causal layer.
· HAMGNN (2026): attention‑based meta‑learning for drug repurposing; lacks context and causal inference.
· KARMA (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight): a general multi‑agent LLM framework for KG enrichment; extracts flat triples only, lacks disease‑specific adaptation and single‑cell or causal layers.
· TxGNN (Nature Medicine, 2024): a graph foundation model for zero‑shot drug repurposing trained on 17,080 diseases, achieving 49.2% improvement in indication prediction and 35.1% in contraindication prediction over eight baselines including HGT. It remains flat‑triple, without hallucination auditing, causal pruning, single‑cell nodes, or continual learning — but it is the current field standard for repurposing benchmarks.

All of these systems store biological facts as flat triples — (subject, predicate, object) — losing the conditions under which a fact holds. As demonstrated by Wang et al. (2026), “Junctin activates RyR [at low luminal Ca²⁺]” and “Junctin inhibits RyR [at high luminal Ca²⁺]” become a logical contradiction when flattened. Their AutoBioKG pipeline proposed composite triples enriched with contextual attributes. Our context‑quadruple schema — (Entity, Relation, Entity, Context, Source) — extends this approach and will be the fundamental data model of the entire PhD.

A perfect PD example of the need for context arises from the LRRK2–GBA1 interaction. A March 2026 study (npj Parkinson’s Disease) demonstrated that LRRK2 kinase activity mediates an increase in lysosomal GCase activity in response to IFNγ stimulation in iPSC‑derived microglia, while the same LRRK2 activity is associated with decreased GCase activity in dopaminergic neurons carrying GBA1 mutations. The flat triple “LRRK2 → affects → GCase activity” is ambiguous and contradictory unless conditioned on [cell type: iPSC‑microglia, condition: IFNγ] vs. [cell type: DA neuron, background: GBA1 mutation]. This real‑world 2026 contradiction will serve as a central validation case for our context‑conditioned splitting.

1.5 Hallucination in Biomedical Language Models

Large language models are prone to generating factually incorrect statements; in biomedical domains, hallucination rates exceed 60%. Defenses include hybrid LLM+KG systems (Müller et al., 2025), ontology‑grounded GraphRAG (Lee & Park, 2026), and knowledge‑graph‑based hallucination benchmarks such as KGHaluBench (2026). No PD‑specific extraction pipeline has integrated rigorous, dual‑LLM adversarial verification with grounding to primary literature. This thesis proposes to build and benchmark such an auditor.

1.6 Causal Inference from Multi‑Omics Data

Observational correlations can be misleading. Mendelian randomisation (MR) uses genetic variants as instrumental variables to infer causal relationships between molecular traits and disease. Gu et al. (2023) used multi‑omics MR with colocalization and Steiger filtering to identify GPNMB and CD38 as PD risk factors, but their instrument set was limited to earlier GWAS. The GP2 Mega‑GWAS now provides 1.8 million samples and 134 risk loci, greatly expanding the instrument pool. GPNMB has been confirmed as a causal risk mediator: its shed extracellular domain binds α‑synuclein fibrils and promotes cell‑to‑cell spread, and anti‑GPNMB antibodies block this in preclinical models (Neuron, 2026; npj PD, 2026). No existing PD knowledge graph incorporates MR‑based causal pruning, let alone using the latest mega‑GWAS.

1.7 New Drug Target: α‑Synuclein–ClpP

A 2026 study demonstrated that α‑synuclein binds the mitochondrial protease ClpP via its NAC domain, impairing ClpP activity and causing proteotoxic stress. A rationally designed peptide, CS2, disrupted this interaction and rescued mitochondrial function in PD patient iPSC‑derived neurons (PMC12751141). This newly validated protein–protein interaction is absent from all current PD knowledge graphs and provides an ideal internal benchmark: a system that cannot independently discover such a connection from the literature and structural data is not fit for purpose.

1.8 Continual Learning Under Coherent Updates

Knowledge graphs must evolve. Simply retraining a GNN on new data causes catastrophic forgetting. Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC) reduces forgetting from ~12.6% to ~6.85% on KG tasks, but forgetting increases when tasks are semantically coherent. Prompt‑Aware EWC (PA‑EWC, arXiv 2511.20732) addresses this by categorising parameters and applying adaptive consolidation strengths, reducing forgetting by an additional 17.58% on medical imaging tasks. Meanwhile, the LLM‑based SimGCL (arXiv 2505.18697) has shown that language model representations can mitigate graph continual learning forgetting. A PD knowledge graph will be updated with streams of semantically coherent literature; this thesis will implement and evaluate PA‑EWC against both vanilla EWC and SimGCL.

