4. Proposed Methodology

All work described below is planned. No part of the system has been built, and no data has been collected. This section outlines the methods, rationale, and expected challenges.

4.1 Data Acquisition and Versioned Ingestion

We will source multi‑omics and clinical data from public, de‑identified cohorts: PPMI (whole‑genome sequencing, blood transcriptomics, CSF proteomics, methylation arrays, metabolomics, longitudinal scores); AMP‑PD (WGS, RNA‑seq, harmonised longitudinal plasma/CSF proteomics); GP2 Mega‑GWAS summary statistics (1.8 M samples); single‑cell and spatial data (snRNA‑seq/snATAC‑seq from post‑mortem PD midbrain and cortex, organoid scRNA‑seq from Zagare, Visium, GeoMx, and MERFISH datasets including the fully confirmed Ma et al. Nature Communications 2025 spatial study); curated databases (STRING, Reactome, GO, OpenTargets, DrugBank, ChEMBL); and the PubMed Central Open Access subset.

A harmonisation agent will map all entities to standard ontologies (HGNC, MONDO, Cell Ontology, Biolink Model). Every database import will be recorded with version number, import date, and DOI to enable version‑locked snapshots essential for retrospective validation.

4.2 The Context‑Quadruple Schema

The central knowledge representation is a quadruple:
(Entity_1, Relation, Entity_2, Context, Source)

Context is a structured field capturing cell type, genetic background, species, assay conditions, spatial region, dose, time point, and environmental modifiers. This design follows AutoBioKG and directly resolves contradictions: the conflicting flat triples about LRRK2 and GCase activity become condition‑specific quadruples:

· LRRK2 → increases → GCase activity [cell type: iPSC‑derived microglia, condition: IFNγ stimulation]
· LRRK2 → decreases → GCase activity [cell type: dopaminergic neuron, background: GBA1 mutation]

The extraction pipeline comprises:

· Literature Extraction Agent: a fine‑tuned biomedical LM processes full‑text articles using multi‑level segmentation, entity recognition (PubTator, custom NER), relation extraction (BioBERT fine‑tuned on BioRED and ChemProt), cross‑paragraph coreference, and few‑shot GPT‑4o‑mini prompts to fill context fields with explicit experimental metadata.
· Biological Relationship Agent: integrates database relationships, maps tissue/cell annotations into Context fields, and adds cross‑omic edges (e.g., eQTLs).
· Hallucination Auditor: two independent LLMs (Meditron‑70B, Llama‑3‑70B) are prompted to verify: “Does the following quadruple accurately reflect the provided article text? Verify all entities, the relation, and every context detail. Answer YES only if fully supported.” A quadruple is accepted only if both agree; disagreements are flagged for manual review. A gold‑standard PD test set of 200 curated quadruples will be created to measure precision, recall, F1, and fabrication rate. The auditor’s performance will be compared against Müller et al.’s hybrid system, Lee & Park’s GraphRAG, KGHaluBench, and KARMA’s single‑agent verification.

4.3 Multi‑Agent Architecture

The system consists of nine specialised agents orchestrated around a central Neo4j graph database.

· Agent 1 — Literature Extraction Agent: extracts candidate quadruples from full‑text articles.
· Agent 2 — Biological Relationship Agent: integrates database imports, builds the multi‑omic graph, and adds structural edges.
· Agent 3 — Hallucination Auditing Agent: performs dual‑LLM verification and benchmarking.
· Agent 4 — Pattern Detection Agent (Endotype Discovery): A Heterogeneous Graph Transformer (HGT) with relation‑type‑specific attention and temporal edge encoding will be trained to produce patient embeddings. Patient nodes are initialised with multi‑omics vectors; gene nodes with BioBERT embeddings; cell‑type nodes with averaged marker gene expression. To link patient bulk data to single‑cell populations, we will employ cell‑type proportion deconvolution and map patient‑level genetic risk scores onto cell‑type‑specific regulatory networks. Training objectives are link prediction and contrastive learning for patients with similar clinical trajectories. Leiden clustering on the patient embedding k‑NN graph yields endotype clusters, enriched for vulnerable neuron subtypes, glial states, and MIRO1‑R272Q‑related mitochondrial signatures. A prodromal risk index is derived from embedding proximity to the ANXA1⁺‑loss cluster. Comparison will be made against BioMGNN v2 and Zagare et al.’s organoid‑based stratification.
· Agent 5 — Causal Inference Agent: instruments are selected from GP2 Mega‑GWAS and eQTL/pQTL studies. Two‑sample MR (IVW, MR‑Egger, weighted median), colocalization (COLOC, PP.H4 > 0.8), Steiger directionality test, and pleiotropy filtering are applied. A composite causal confidence score (0–1) is assigned to each molecular‑to‑disease edge; edges below 0.3 are pruned. GPNMB will be recovered as a risk mediator.
· Agent 6 — Contradiction Management Agent: detects contradictory edge pairs by predicate semantics and embedding similarity. When Context fields differ, edges are split into condition‑specific forms. Identical contexts with opposing relations are flagged and fed into a hidden‑moderator prediction classifier. Evaluation uses HealthContradict as a gold standard and compares with KARMA’s conflict edge reduction.
· Agent 7 — Hypothesis Generation Agent: performs limited‑depth path search on the pruned, context‑resolved KG and composes natural‑language hypotheses with explicit evidence chains, effect direction, and a calibrated Bayesian posterior probability (see 4.4).
· Agent 8 — Drug Repurposing Agent (Extended): maps hypothesis targets to DrugBank/ChEMBL/OpenTargets, filters by CNS MPO score and safety data, and ranks candidates by a repurposing score combining causal confidence, drug‑target affinity, and clinical phase. The primary benchmark is TxGNN, the current field standard for zero‑shot drug repurposing, evaluating AUPRC for indications and contraindications. HAMGNN will be used as a secondary comparison, especially for its meta‑learning strategy on sparse diseases. Expected candidates include anti‑GPNMB antibodies, omaveloxolone, GT‑02287, and BIA 28‑6156.
· Agent 9 — Validation Agent: performs retrospective validation (pre‑2010 snapshot must rank LRRK2 and GBA in top 5% and predict αSyn–ClpP interaction). In‑silico validation via Connectivity Map. Defines an hMLO protocol for top hypothesis testing (iPSC lines, treatment, scRNA‑seq, pS129 α‑synuclein, mitochondrial function endpoints).

4.4 Calibrated Bayesian Confidence Model

Every hypothesis is assigned a posterior probability:
P(H | E, C) ∝ P(E | H, C) × P(H | C)

· Prior P(H|C): incorporates genetic support (colocalization posterior), interaction confidence (STRING), clinical consistency, replication evidence, and a small novelty bonus.
· Likelihood P(E|H,C): modelled via logistic regression on evidence features (causal confidence, structural ipTM, literature co‑occurrence), trained on a calibration set of known true/false PD relationships.
· Calibration evaluation: Expected Calibration Error (ECE) computed on a held‑out retrospective set. We compare against four uncertainty quantification frameworks:
  · BKG2U: transforms SemMedDB into an uncertain KG by scoring supporting sentences; it operates on flat triples, whereas our system is the first to apply uncertainty quantification to quadruples where the Context field itself has factuality variance.
  · MedBayes‑Lite: focuses on LLM/transformer calibration.
  · Ca2KG (WWW 2026): a causality‑aware calibration framework for KG‑RAG, directly relevant because our hypothesis scoring guides downstream retrieval and reasoning; it is the closest architectural comparator.
  · UAG (AAAI 2025): incorporates conformal prediction into KG‑LLM reasoning to provide formal coverage guarantees, complementing our empirical ECE metric with a theoretically grounded approach.
    Monte Carlo dropout will provide predictive uncertainty from the GNN and LLM components.

4.5 Continual Learning via PA‑EWC

To incorporate new PD literature and clinical trial results without forgetting:

· PA‑EWC: parameters are categorised as text‑extraction, graph‑structural, or medical‑semantic using prompt‑guided specialisation, with adaptive consolidation strength λ (higher for medical‑semantic).
· Task interleaving: updates from different omics layers are interleaved to reduce semantic coherence penalty.
· Memory replay: 5% of historical quadruples stored for rehearsal.
· Benchmarks: Vanilla EWC and SimGCL (LLM‑based graph continual learning). Sequential update benchmark: Task 1 = pre‑2010; Task 2 = 2010–2015; Task 3 = 2016–2021; Task 4 = 2022–2026. Target forgetting ≤ 4%, representing >15% relative improvement over EWC.

4.6 Structural Layer with AlphaFold3 and ColabFold

The α‑synuclein–ClpP interaction and other key complexes (GPNMB, TPP1, LRRK2) will be docked using AlphaFold3 for high‑accuracy prediction of small complexes, and ColabFold with templates to generate larger pools of predictions (AF3 produces only 5 models per complex). A recent benchmarking study (Genz et al., 2025) confirmed that ipTM and model confidence best discriminate correct from incorrect heterodimeric predictions; we will use ipTM > 0.8 to create “structurally_compatible” edges in the graph, providing orthogonal evidence for interaction hypotheses.

