7. Expected Contributions (Theoretical)

Upon completion, this PhD will deliver:

1. The first context‑quadruple knowledge graph for Parkinson’s disease (and for any neurodegenerative disease), incorporating spatial transcriptomics, glial cell types, and the MIRO1‑R272Q mitochondrial endotype.
2. A rigorously benchmarked hallucination auditor for biomedical quadruple extraction, with an open‑source PD gold‑standard test set.
3. A causally‑pruned PD graph using the largest PD GWAS to date, demonstrating that causal filtering alters the graph’s topology and hypothesis ranking.
4. Single‑cell‑anchored endotypes that connect cellular vulnerability directly to patient stratification, including a prodromal risk index and deconvolution‑based integration of bulk and single‑cell data.
5. A calibrated Bayesian hypothesis scoring system with published ECE metrics and comparisons to four complementary uncertainty frameworks, establishing the first quadruple‑schema confidence assessment.
6. A demonstration of PA‑EWC for PD knowledge graph updates, showing that continual learning is feasible on semantically coherent biomedical literature streams.
7. Independent rediscovery of the α‑synuclein–ClpP interaction and GPNMB risk mediation, serving as rigorous internal validations.
8. A drug repurposing pipeline benchmarked against the field standard TxGNN, and a detailed organoid‑based validation protocol.
9. An entirely open‑source toolkit — graph snapshots, extracted quadruples, auditor benchmarks, and model weights — released for the PD community.

All of these contributions are prospective; no part of the system has yet been built. This proposal serves as the theoretical blueprint.

