Appendix C: Detailed Agent Specifications

Agent 1 — Literature Extraction Agent

· Input: Full‑text biomedical articles (PubMed Central XML, PDF converted via Grobid).
· Processing steps: Sentence segmentation → entity recognition (PubTator3 API + custom fine‑tuned BioBERT on BioRED) → relation extraction (BioBERT‑based RE model fine‑tuned on ChemProt, DDI, BioRED) → cross‑paragraph coreference resolution using span‑based model → context field filling via few‑shot prompting of GPT‑4o‑mini.
· Output: Candidate quadruples with span offsets, confidence scores for each field.
· Failure mode handling: Sentences with ambiguous references deferred to manual review queue.

Agent 2 — Biological Relationship Agent

· Input: Curated database dumps (STRING v12.0, Reactome 2025, Gene Ontology 2025‑01, OpenTargets 25.02, DrugBank 6.0, ChEMBL 34).
· Processing: Entity mapping to HGNC/MONDO using OGER++ and custom dictionary. Relation augmentation: adds tissue specificity from STRING to Context, gene‑disease associations from OpenTargets, drug‑target links from DrugBank. Cross‑omic links (eQTL from GTEx v10, pQTL from deCODE/Fenland) added as specific edge types.
· Output: Database‑sourced quadruples with version tags.

Agent 3 — Hallucination Auditing Agent

· LLMs: Meditron‑70B (medical domain) and Llama‑3‑70B‑Instruct (general), served via vLLM on 4×A100 GPUs.
· Prompt template (both models): “You are a rigorous biomedical fact-checker. Read the following text excerpt and the proposed quadruple. Determine if the quadruple is entirely supported by the text. Check all entities, the relation, directionality, and all context details (cell type, condition, etc.). Answer ONLY ‘YES’ or ‘NO’ and provide a brief justification.”
· Consensus: Both must answer YES for acceptance; single NO flags for human review; dual NO rejects.
· Gold standard: 200 quadruples manually curated by two annotators (Cohen’s κ target ≥ 0.8). 100 from PD literature, 100 from general biomedical to test domain specificity.

Agent 4 — Pattern Detection Agent (Endotype Discovery)

· Model: Heterogeneous Graph Transformer (HGT) implemented in PyTorch Geometric.
· Node features: Patient nodes initialised with concatenated multi‑omics vectors (PCs of transcriptomics, proteomics, methylation); gene nodes with BioBERT‑base embeddings; cell‑type nodes with averaged expression of top 100 marker genes from Kamath et al. and Wang et al.
· Edge types: patient‑has_variant‑gene, gene‑encodes‑protein, protein‑interacts‑protein [Context], protein‑in_pathway‑pathway, patient‑has_expression‑gene, cell_type‑expresses‑gene, spatial_region‑contains‑cell_type, etc.
· Training: Link prediction objective (reconstruct held‑out patient‑gene edges) + contrastive loss using clinical trajectory similarity (MDS‑UPDRS III slope).
· Endotype clustering: After training, extract patient embeddings, construct k‑NN graph (k=20), apply Leiden algorithm, test cluster stability via bootstrapping.
· Validation: Enrichment for cell‑type markers, gene set enrichment (fGSEA), differential progression rates (linear mixed models), prodromal index AUC.

Agent 5 — Causal Inference Agent

· Instrument selection: From GP2 Mega‑GWAS: independent (LD r²<0.001, clump‑r²=0.1, window=1000 kb) genome‑wide significant (p<5e‑8) variants. For eQTL/pQTL, cis‑variants within 1 Mb of gene start/end.
· MR methods: Inverse‑variance weighted (IVW), MR‑Egger, weighted median, simple mode, weighted mode (TwoSampleMR R package). Heterogeneity assessed via Cochran’s Q; pleiotropy via MR‑Egger intercept.
· Colocalization: COLOC (R package) with default priors; PP.H4 > 0.8 considered strong evidence of shared causal variant.
· Steiger test: Computed per variant to confirm exposure → outcome direction; only variants passing Steiger (p<0.05) retained.
· Composite causal score: Weighted average of MR consistency (50%), colocalization probability (30%), and Steiger confirmation (20%). Edges with score < 0.3 pruned.

Agent 6 — Contradiction Management Agent

· Detection: For each new edge, search existing edges with same subject‑object pair but opposing relation predicates (using VerbNet polarity and custom biomedical predicate opposition list). Embedding cosine similarity > 0.8 for contextual meaning flags potential contradiction.
· Resolution: If Context fields differ, split into distinct quadruples and annotate with the differing condition. If Context identical, flag as unresolved, add to hidden‑moderator training set.
· Hidden‑moderator predictor: A multi‑label classifier trained on unresolved contradictions using features derived from the sentence context, metadata, and subject‑object embeddings, to predict potential missing context variables.
· Evaluation: HealthContradict benchmark; proportion of correctly context‑split examples; comparison of raw conflict edges before/after splitting vs. KARMA.

Agent 7 — Hypothesis Generation Agent

· Path search: BFS from a disease node (e.g., “Parkinson’s disease”) to a drug node with depth ≤ 4 edges, constrained to causal edges (score ≥ 0.3) and structurally compatible edges (ipTM > 0.8). Paths filtered by logical consistency and absence of contradictory context.
· Hypothesis composition: For each path, generate a templated NL statement: “In [cell type / condition], [source entity] [relation] [target entity], which [downstream effect], thus [drug] may [therapeutic effect] for PD patients with [endotype].”
· Confidence: Bayesian posterior computed per Section 4.4.

Agent 8 — Drug Repurposing Agent

· Mapping: Hypothesis target proteins mapped to DrugBank IDs, ChEMBL compounds with activity < 1 μM. CNS MPO ≥ 4 considered CNS‑penetrant.
· Ranking score: Repurposing Score = 0.4×causal_confidence + 0.3×normalised_drug_target_affinity + 0.2×clinical_phase_bonus (Phase 3 = 1, Phase 2 = 0.7, etc.) + 0.1×safety_penalty (black‑box warnings).
· Benchmark: Primary: TxGNN zero‑shot predictions on PD‑related indications/contraindications (AUPRC). Secondary: HAMGNN meta‑learning NDCG@10 on sparse disease subsets (e.g., GBA‑PD).

Agent 9 — Validation Agent

· Retrospective snapshot: Load all database versions and literature published ≤ Dec 2009. Run entire pipeline; assess ranking of LRRK2, GBA in top 5% of gene‑disease edges; compute if ClpP is among top‑5 novel SNCA interactors (not in training, predicted via structural + text evidence).
· Connectivity Map validation: For top drug candidates, query Connectivity Map (CLUE) to check if drug‑induced gene expression signatures inversely match PD disease signatures.
· hMLO protocol: Detailed design (cell lines, differentiation, treatments, readouts) described in Appendix G.

